function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><div class=\"doc-title\"><h2>7. Arrays and structs</h2></div><div class=\"doc-section content\"><p>Use an array for an ordered collection of one type. Use a struct when fields need names.</p><h2>Arrays</h2><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><strong>Output</strong></p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Ada\n</code></pre><p>Array indexes start at zero. An invalid index traps.</p><p>Declare the item type when an array starts empty:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(&quot;Ada&quot;)\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>reserve</code>, and <code>resize</code> change an array.</p><h2>Array values</h2><p>An array is a mutable reference value inside one workspace:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function append_three(values : [s64]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], &quot; &quot;, second[2], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>Assignment and parameter passing share the array identity. Returns and captures also share it. Content mutation through a parameter affects the caller. A parameter binding remains immutable, so <code>values = [3]</code> is invalid. Array growth preserves identity.</p><p>A <code>for</code> loop reads array items in order:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&q")
    print("uot;]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, &quot;: &quot;, name, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n    }\n}\n</code></pre><p>The optional index follows the value and has type <code>s64</code>. The loop rechecks the current array length. It safely stops after shrinkage and can visit appended items. The loop retains each current managed item while the body runs.</p><p>The loop item is a value, not an assignable array position. Use an index to replace an item.</p><h2>Structs</h2><p>A struct defines a named fixed layout:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, &quot; &quot;, point.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>The generated constructor takes fields in declaration order. A struct instance is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity. Struct fields are assignable:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>The field mutation affects the caller. The parameter binding itself remains immutable.</p><p>A receiver-first function can use method syntax:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -&gt; point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>This is the same call as <code>total(point)</code>.</p><h2>Inspect a struct</h2><p>Use <code>value::size</code> to get the number of declared fields. U")
    print("se <code>value::items</code> when code must inspect fields by name.</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Build {\n    id : s64\n    status : string\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var build := Build(s64(9007199254740993), &quot;ready&quot;)\n    var fields := build::items\n    var key := &quot;status&quot;\n    print(build::type_name, &quot; &quot;, build::size, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n    print(fields[key].type_name, &quot; &quot;, string(fields[key].value), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><strong>Output</strong></p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Build 2\nstring ready\n</code></pre><p><code>value::items</code> returns a <code>dval</code> map keyed by field name. Each field entry has <code>value</code> and <code>type_name</code> keys. Use <code>fields.id.value</code> for a fixed field name. Use <code>fields[key][&quot;value&quot;]</code> for a dynamic field name.</p><p>The reflected field value is a <code>dval</code>. Convert it to the static type before using it in typed code. The struct reference page defines nested values, copying, and callable fields.</p><h2>Choose the right shape</h2><p>Use an array when every item has one static type. Use a struct for application data with a stable shape. Use a <code>dval</code> map or list for decoded input and data that crosses a Bearer boundary.</p></div><nav class=\"guide-navigation\" aria-label=\"Capy guide navigation\"><div class=\"guide-previous\">Previous: <a href=\"/doc/guide/strings-and-markup/\">6. Strings and HTML</a></div><div class=\"guide-index\"><a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div><div class=\"guide-next\">Next: <a href=\"/doc/guide/dynamic-values/\">8. Dynamic values</a></div></nav></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/install-and-first-program/\">1. Getting sta")
    print("rted</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/source-structure-and-syntax/\">2. Basic syntax</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/values-and-types/\">3. Types</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/expressions-and-control-flow/\">4. Expressions and control flow</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/functions-and-closures/\">5. Functions</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/strings-and-markup/\">6. Strings and HTML</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/dynamic-values/\">8. Dynamic values</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/web-handlers-and-requests/\">9. Handling a web request</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/units-components-and-exports/\">10. Units and components</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/tasks-and-jobs/\">11. Tasks and jobs</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/errors-testing-and-style/\">12. Errors and testing</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/coming-from-react/\">13. Coming from React</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/struct/\">struct</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/get-type-name/\">get_type_name</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/length/\">length</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/set/\">set</a></aside></div>")
}
